raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Among American X/Twitter users, are the people who express hostility in political exchanges also the people who score highly on measures of democratic engagement and its antecedents?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We examine hostile expression in political exchanges on X/Twitter and ask whether\nsuch expression is associated with the same forms of engagement that are typically viewed as central to democratic\ncitizenship.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is the association between engagement and hostility stronger among users whose feelings about the two parties’ leaders are more polarized?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Second, we provide suggestive analyses of whether these associations differ by affective polarization [22],\nmeasured as polarized affect towards political leaders.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do politically hostile users have more opportunities to be heard, as indicated by how often they post and the size of their X/Twitter networks?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Expectation 3. Online political hostility is associated with greater activity and potential reach on X/Twitter, measured by\nposting frequency and numbers of friends and followers.\nWe treat this final expectation as exploratory in nature given the lack of existing research.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Accounts of online political hostility need to consider engaged citizens, not just people experiencing marginalization or exhibiting antagonistic traits. The same interest, knowledge, and participation valued in democratic life are also associated with hostile political expression.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These\nfindings suggest that online political hostility is not only associated with deprivation, marginalization, or personality-based\nexplanations, as highlighted in prior work [14,15]. It is also associated with forms of engagement that are often considered\ncentral to democratic citizenship.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Who produces hostile speech may help explain its visibility: even apart from any attention a hostile post attracts, its author tends to post more and have a larger potential audience.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This suggests that hostile political\nexpression may be more visible on X/Twitter not only because hostile content attracts attention, but also because users\nwho express such hostility are more active and have greater potential reach [3].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The practical implication is conditional: changes to platforms alone may be insufficient if hostility is bound up with partisan animus and the conduct of political leaders.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "However, if online political hostility is also associated with political engagement and out-party animus, platform-level interventions alone may be insufficient.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Reducing hostile political\nengagement online may also require attention to broader political polarization and the role of political elites",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The researchers linked a US YouGov panel to X accounts over six survey waves, recruiting initially in January 2019 and adding panelists in September 2020. They linked 2,567 users to surveys; X-only analyses included up to 3,643 users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All participants were recruited through YouGov’s standing web panel in the United States.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "We recruited the original set of panelists in January 2019.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "These\nparticipants, along with an additional set of panelists who were included to increase the study’s power in September 2020,\nwere then asked to participate in five additional survey waves, administered at various time points until the final survey in\nJanuary 2021.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The total number of panelists where we were able to link survey responses to their X handle in this period\nwas 2567. For the analyses where we only use X data the maximum number of respondents is 3643.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The behavioral record consisted of more than 9.25 million public posts collected with participants’ consent. The unit of analysis was the user, not the post.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "to provide their X ID and give consent to let us scrape their publicly available X data and link it to their survey answers.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "The scraping of X commenced on March 4, 2019 and stopped in January 2021. During this period, we managed to\nscrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists. All measures based on X data are aggregated at the level of the individual\npanelist since our unit of analysis is individual panelists and not posts.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The primary hostility measure used word2vec embeddings learned from panelists’ posts. The researchers scored each post by its cosine similarity to a combined political-hate vector, then averaged scores by user or user-period.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we create word vectors based on posts from our panelists using the word2vec method [61,62].",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, we combine the word vectors\n“political” and “hate” into a combined vector that captures the co-occurrence of political and hostile language in the corpus.\nThird, we use this combined measure to calculate the cosine similarity between individual posts and the “political hate”\nword vector:",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Fourth and finally, we create a\nmeasure of a respondent’s general level of political hostility by averaging the hostility scores of all their posts; for the panel\nmodels we compute this average per time unit investigated (wave or month, depending on the analysis).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Validation included a comparison with human judgments on 2,469 posts. On a held-out test set, the dichotomized embedding score achieved 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The SU approach was also validated against 2,469 hand-labeled posts rated by four expert raters using the definition\nof “Political Hostility” as: “Degrading, harassing and intimidating online content grounded in political differences.” [60].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Here, it reached 74% accuracy against a 50% baseline when dichotomized on a held-out test set.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Robustness checks used a sentiment dictionary, a general toxicity score, the political and hate components of the embedding separately, and a supervised political-hostility classifier trained on hand-labeled posts.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We estimate these associations using our primary measure of online political hostility and\nthe two secondary robustness measures, AFINN sentiment scores and Perspective API toxicity scores.",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "Fig 2 provides a replication of the analyses above where the composite dependent variable of online political hostility is\nsubdivided into the two components of “political” and “hate” (i.e., general hostility). In addition, as described above, we\nalso use an alternative supervised classifier of online political hostility based on a sample of hand labelled posts.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Surveys measured education, perceived political competence, interest, factual knowledge, and the frequency of political participation online and offline. Interest was self-rated, whereas knowledge was scored from correct answers.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "From the surveys, we obtained individual-level measures of respondents’\neducation, sense of internal efficacy, political knowledge, political interest, self-reported participation in offline settings,\nand self-reported participation in online settings.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Political interest is measured using a standard American National Election Studies (ANES) question asking respondents\nabout their level of political interest on a five-point scale ranging from “Extremely interested” to “Not interested at all.”\nPolitical knowledge is measured using five factual questions from the ANES, scored as the number of correct\nanswers.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Leader-directed polarization was the difference between a respondent’s average warmth ratings for three leaders of their own party and three of the opposing party. The authors examined both high/low groups and formal interactions, adjusting the latter for age and gender.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Respondents rated three Republican politicians (Ted Cruz, Donald Trump,\nand Mike Pence) and three Democratic politicians (Joe Biden, Elizabeth Warren, and Bernie Sanders) on a 0–100 feeling\nthermometer. We used respondents’ party identification to distinguish in-party from out-party leaders and calculated the\ndifference between average in-party and out-party leader ratings.",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Fig 4 displays the associations separately for users above and below the midpoint\nof the leader-directed affective-polarization scale.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Formal interaction coefficients between indicators of political engagement, its antecedents, and leader-directed affective polarization.\nAll models control for age and gender but not the full set of controls to reduce problems of multicollinearity.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The cross-sectional analyses pooled observations across waves, accounting for repeated respondents and missing survey measures. The main models adjusted for broad personality traits, demographics, and party identification.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "For the cross-sectional analyses, we use ordinary least squares (OLS) regression with cluster robust standard errors to\naccount for the fact that a single X user can participate across multiple waves.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "This dataset is organized as a Time-Series\nCross-Section (TSCS) dataset using pooled OLS. Not all measures are available in each survey wave and we therefore\nuse multiple imputation and full information maximum likelihood to estimate missing values in these analyses.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "All models control for the Big\nFive personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For the temporal analysis, random-intercept cross-lagged panel models distinguished changes in an individual user’s online behavior from stable differences between users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To examine whether the same pattern appears in\ntemporal within-person models, we use RI-CLPMs with behavioral measures of online political engagement and online\nhostility.",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "For the panel analyses, we use Random-Intercept Cross-Lagged Panel Models (RI-CLPM). These models separate\nstable between-person differences from within-person change",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors checked for multiple testing within four families of models using false-discovery-rate correction and Bonferroni-adjusted intervals.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define four separate families of tests: the primary association models,\nthe robustness outcome models, the affective-polarization interaction models, and the exploratory activity/reach models.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "Within each family we apply a Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate (FDR) correction and, for the more conservative\nreading requested by the reviewers, also report Bonferroni-adjusted 95% confidence intervals.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Exploratory negative-binomial models compared counts of posts, accounts followed, and followers. The latter two distinguish a user’s outgoing connections from their potential audience.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The\nexploratory activity and reach models are an exception: these are negative-binomial count models, and their estimates are\nreported on the original count scale.",
            "page": 10
          },
          {
            "text": "We investigate the visibility of politically hostile users by first showing results from count models that examine whether\nthose who are politically hostile (a) post more, (b) have more friends, and (c) have more followers. A “friend” in X’s usage\nis someone I follow, and a “follower” is someone who follows me.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Political interest, knowledge, and online participation had the strongest positive associations with political hostility. The pattern persisted after adjustment for personality, demographics, and party identification.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the\nstrongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "The associations were obtained after controlling for\nbroad personality traits, income, age, gender, and party identification.",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The association was not simply an artifact of engaged users discussing politics more often. It persisted when the analysis held constant each user’s share of political posts and when hostility was measured in other ways.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "if anything the results are slightly stronger when controlling for the share of political posts. This suggests that the results are not simply driven by more politically engaged users posting more about politics.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The robustness analyses are broadly consistent with the main pattern, with significant and sizeable associations across\nall three measures.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Polarized feelings toward party leaders most clearly strengthened the associations for online and offline participation. Only eight of eighteen interaction terms survived false-discovery-rate correction; the pattern did not apply uniformly to engagement and its antecedents.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "However, the moderation evidence is not uniform across all indicators. Formal interaction models (Fig 3) show\nthe clearest support for online and offline participation,",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "of the eighteen interaction terms, eight\nremain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "When a user became more politically engaged online, higher hostility tended to follow on all three measures. There was weaker, less consistent evidence that hostility also preceded increased engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement\nare followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures. We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this\npattern is weaker and less consistent.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "More hostile users posted more, had more followers, and, to a lesser degree, followed more accounts. Comparing mean hostility with 1.5 standard deviations above it, estimated posts rose from about 420 to 900 and followers from about 220 to 370.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers\nof followers and, to a lesser extent, friends.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above\nthe mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the\nnumber of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The outcome is hostility grounded in political differences. It excludes nonpolitical toxic exchanges and is broader than hate speech against a particular group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We define online political hostility as the expression of degrading, malicious, and\nintimidating online behavior towards individuals or collectives based on political\ndifferences.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Accordingly, toxic online exchanges in the aftermath of, say, a\nsports game fall outside the purview of what we examine here.",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "we do not wish to confuse this more general measure of online political hostility with the narrower concept of “hate speech” which we are not purporting to measure.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The main sample comes from a deliberately chosen, highly polarized US setting on a public, text-based platform. The findings need not transfer to other platforms or countries; a supplementary Danish X/Twitter analysis offers only tentative evidence of broader applicability.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The data collection focused on the United States specifically because the associations of interest may be especially\nvisible in highly polarized political environments.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "This platform is a public, textbased, and highly political platform, and the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances, audiences, moderation systems, or norms of interaction.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Some tentative evidence of broader applicability is provided in S1 Appendix, where online political hostility in a sample of\nDanish X/Twitter users is also positively associated with users’ political interest, internal efficacy, and news consumption,",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The observational results do not show that increasing engagement causes hostility. Even within-person comparisons cannot exclude changing common causes, measurement error, or feedback in the opposite direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The RI-CLPM analyses reduce concerns about stable time-invariant confounding\nby focusing on within-person change, but they do not rule out time-varying confounding, reciprocal relationships, or measurement error. Political sophistication or partisan involvement may be common causes of both participation and hostility.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not identify causal pathways among the engagement indicators. In particular, it does not show that education makes people more hostile independently of their greater engagement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Third, and relatedly, the present findings are mute on the exact causal relationships between the different indicators of political engagement and their antecedents.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the analyses should not be taken to imply\nthat, for example, more educated users are more hostile absent their generally higher levels of political engagement.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Validation does not make the embedding score a perfect measure: language about political conflict can resemble hostile language without itself expressing hostility.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measure may partly capture\nproximity to politically salient conflict language or hate-related political vocabulary rather than hostility alone.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The polarization findings concern feelings toward leaders, not feelings toward parties or their ordinary supporters, which were not measured in parallel.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This measure captures affect toward party elites rather than affect toward parties as institutions or ordinary party\nsupporters, as is standard in research on affective polarization. The survey did not include parallel feeling thermometer\nratings of party supporters.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Differences across levels of polarization are descriptive. Polarization could precede or follow hostile interaction, so the interaction estimates do not establish causal moderation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "leader-directed affective\npolarization may be an antecedent, correlate, or consequence of hostile online interaction. We therefore interpret these\nanalyses as suggestive evidence of conditional associations, not as causally identified moderation effects.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Adjusting for the Big Five does not rule out explanations based on sadism, Dark Triad traits, or other specific antagonistic dispositions; the survey did not measure them.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These controls are not intended as a direct test of Dark Triad,\nsadism, or other antagonistic-trait accounts of online hostility. The survey did not include measures of those more specific\ntraits, and we therefore cannot adjudicate those accounts directly.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Posting and follower counts indicate activity and potential reach, not demonstrated influence. The exploratory network plots do not establish who occupies a central position or how communities are structured.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These results suggest greater activity and potential reach, but they\nshould not be interpreted as evidence of formal network influence or centrality.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "For completeness, we include exploratory descriptive\nnetwork visualizations of the study participants’ follower/friend graph in S1 Appendix but do not draw inferences about\ncentrality or community structure.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors combine classical democratic-engagement theory with revisionist work on motivated reasoning and polarization. They extend the revisionist question from how engaged people process political information to how they treat opponents online.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we\nfind that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "We extend this revisionist\nperspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression\non X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The engagement account supplements rather than rejects grievance-based and psychological accounts of online hostility; the authors do not set those explanations against one another.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The present study does not seek to adjudicate between these literatures. Instead, it asks whether online political hostility is also associated with political engagement and its antecedents.",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors retain distinctions from engagement theory rather than treating engagement as a single resource: education and efficacy are preconditions, interest and knowledge reflect motivation and cognition, and participation is behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, education and internal efficacy are distal antecedents of political engagement. Second, political interest and political\nknowledge are motivational and cognitive indicators of engagement. Third, online and offline political participation are\nbehavioral indicators of engagement.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "While some studies investigate the relationships between these different types of indicators and measures [18,19], we will\nin the present study treat them as separate indicators as they may not operate through the same pathways.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper combines engagement and affective-polarization frameworks in a conditional argument: engagement supplies the capacity and opportunity to participate, while partisan feelings may give that participation an antagonistic direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Engagement provides attention, motivation, skills, and opportunities to participate in political discussion. Affective polarization may shape whether such participation is expressed in\nantagonistic terms toward political opponents. Under this logic, the association between engagement and hostility should\nbe stronger among users who evaluate opposing political elites more negatively.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The exploratory reach analysis extends the civic-participation literature’s concern with whose voice is heard to the potential audience of hostile users.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The traditional literature on political engagement often finds that more engaged citizens have greater opportunities to\nmake their voices heard [18,19]. Given the associations above, we therefore turn to our exploratory expectation and\nexamine whether users who express more online political hostility are also more active on X/Twitter and have greater\npotential reach.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Almond and Verba argue that democratic citizenship requires restraint as well as involvement. The authors build on that qualification to the classical case for participation by investigating a possible cost of engagement: hostile political expression.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In particular [20], argued that engagement needed to be tempered with a more parochial relationship to politics such that “the democratic citizen is called on to pursue contradictory\ngoals: he must be active, yet passive; involved, yet not too involved; influential, yet deferential.” ([20], p. 478–479).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "This potential negative side of political engagement is the key focus of\nour empirical investigation.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
